{"gid":"2026_04_MIA_MIN","week":4,"away":"MIA","home":"MIN","away_col":"#2CD3C6","home_col":"#A06BE8","spread":7.5,"total":43.5,"fav":"MIN","hpts":26,"apts":18,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 4 \u00b7 16:05","roof":"Dome","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans MIN.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Aaron Jones</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile MIA throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Malik Washington</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>MIN</strong> host the <strong>MIA</strong> as favorites of <strong>7.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>43.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>MIN 26\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>39% of its snaps with MIN in front</strong>, <strong>35% within one score</strong>, and <strong>26% with MIN chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>MIN should control</strong>. A favorite of 8 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>MIN</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>MIA</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>MIA</strong> to run more total plays than MIN \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for MIA's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Ollie Gordon II</strong> \u2014 MIN are stingy against the run (109 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>T.J. Hockenson</strong> has room to work \u2014 MIA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Caleb Douglas</strong> (WR2) \u2014 MIN leak 53 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p>In the red zone MIN throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jordan Addison</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Malik Washington</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>MIN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>MIN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Aaron Jones</strong> (39 carries, 182 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though MIN's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Jordan Addison</strong> projects for <strong>14 targets and 116 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>T.J. Hockenson</strong> (TE, 12 targets, 91 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Ollie Gordon II</strong>'s rushing projection (19 carries, 59 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Malik Washington</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, MIA funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 62 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 44 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"MIA","col":"#2CD3C6","players":[{"name":"Malik Willis","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":244.7,"book":169.5,"over_th":230.9,"under_th":257.4,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_am":"\u2212115","pover":76,"call":"OVER","ev":42.9,"edge":75.2,"s